---
description: Core architectural standards and engineering guardrails
alwaysApply: true
---
- Separate concerns strictly: HTTP/API handlers, pure domain business logic, and repository/storage interfaces must live in separate files.
- Default to clean in-memory repositories (Maps/dicts) behind explicit interfaces unless a database is explicitly requested. Do NOT install heavy ORMs or unnecessary third-party libraries.
- Use explicit dependency injection so domain services can be unit-tested without starting an HTTP server.
- Enforce strict typing (no `any` in TypeScript; use Pydantic/type hints in Python).
- Handle edge cases defensively: validate all inputs at boundaries, guard against null/missing entities, and return structured domain errors.
- Keep diffs minimal and scoped. Never refactor unrelated files or alter existing response schemas without explicit instruction.
- When asked to create a server and client, add Readme.md with instructions and commands to run them.
- For any new functionality added, add unit tests for the functions and/or API endpoint added with it. And keep a release note in the ReadMe.md with a list of features and brief summary of what they do. Eg, in server, keep a record of API endpoints and what they do, if they call database, if they call any dependency, etc.
- Build iteratively, do not build everything at once.